## 2. Per-paper extraction (full texts)

### 2.1 Attenni et al. 2026, 10.1016/j.suscom.2026.101498 (read in full)
- Title: descriptive noun phrase, 15 words, no colon, no finding. Names three objects (carbon, water, land use) and the lever (spatio-temporal shifting).
- Abstract: 135 words, 6 sentences, single paragraph. Moves: scope ("we investigate"), method with data named (AWS and Azure, FaaS and big-data traces), headline numbers (20% to 85% reduction), secondary finding (temporal less than spatial), robustness (prediction error, seasons). No gap sentence, no implication sentence.
- Sections in order: Introduction; II Background (data-centre impacts); III System model and problem formulation; Methodology (Evaluating environmental footprints, Scheduling methods, Experiment design, Simulation overview, Algorithm 1); Results (Scenario 1 Azure and FaaS, Sensitivity analysis, Scenario 2 AWS and big data); Discussion (two scenarios, comparison, trade-off analysis, Threats to validity); Related work (carbon-aware computing, beyond carbon-aware computing); Conclusion. The roadmap paragraph at the end of the Introduction lists sections by number.
- Figures and tables: 7 figures referenced, 9 tables (I to IX): notation, data per region, VM types, grid-mix sources, impact coefficients, two sensitivity tables, one migration table. Figure captions not extracted (the text render had none), so caption style: not read.
- Results tables: sensitivity tables give the improvement versus the Local baseline as mean and std per season and per forecast-error level (MAE 0.05 to 0.2), for carbon, water, land use. Signs matter (some negative). No confidence intervals, no tests. Bold best: not visible in the text render.
- Contribution wording: three bullets; "We propose a holistic formulation...", "We provide a systematic evaluation...", and "We release all code and data from this study" (quote). First person plural, active.
- Baseline: one, the Local (run where requested) baseline. No oracle. No simple-policy ladder.
- Forecast and runtime handling: forecasts "mimicked using historical data and applying an arbitrary noise distribution"; application runtimes "assumed available to the schedulers"; arrival patterns in the AWS trace synthetically randomised. All three are stated in Threats to validity, item c and b.
- Threats to validity: inside Discussion, lettered a) to d) (resource availability, generality, input data quality, spatial constraints), about 400 words, plain admissions. No separate Limitations section.
- Statistics: mean and std over repeated runs only. Reference count: highest numbered citation is [36] (reference list not in the render).
- Code and data: released (stated in the contributions; footnote link not extracted).

### 2.2 DVFS models for HPC servers, 10.1016/j.suscom.2025.101106 (read in full; authors not extracted, the dataset record names Georges Da Costa)
- Title: descriptive noun phrase, 14 words.
- Abstract: 153 words, 8 sentences. Moves: context (energy in HPC), gap-ish ("bridge theory and practice"), method, validation scale (18 clusters, 9 benchmarks), use case (energy-to-solution). No percentage result in the abstract.
- Sections: Introduction; State of the art (early); Approach; Classical model; Power; Application related power model; Application related performance model; Evaluation methodology; Reproducibility; Evaluation of model quality; Heterogeneity of the error; Naive models; Fluid model; Online detection of constants; Accuracy of online models; Overhead; Conclusion. A dedicated Reproducibility heading is the notable move.
- Figures and tables: about 13 figures detected in the text render (numbered up to 17, so some captions were lost), 5 tables. Caption style: descriptive (what is plotted, which cluster), they do not state findings.
- Metric and baselines: MAPE against measured values; baselines are named naive models (Naive rand, Naive all app) beside the proposed Fluid model; measurement precision given as standard deviation per cluster. No bootstrap, no tests.
- Limitations: a short paragraph inside the Conclusion (single-server scope, need for micro-architecture values); no separate section.
- Data: Zenodo dataset DOI 10.5281/zenodo.10982238 cited in the references. Reference count: not counted.

